## **The Solution: The Digital Nutrition System**

### **Pillar 1: Digital Food Groups (The Macronutrients)**

Instead of "good apps" vs "bad apps," ScreenDiet classifies all digital consumption into **four macronutrients** based on neurological impact:

| **Digital Macronutrient** | **Definition** | **Examples** | **Metabolic Effect** | **Daily Target** |
|---------------------------|----------------|--------------|----------------------|------------------|
| **Protein (Creation)** | Apps where you produce, not consume | Notes, Camera, GarageBand, Code editors, Writing apps | Builds "attention muscle," delayed dopamine, satisfaction | 30% of screen time |
| **Carbs (Consumption)** | Passive entertainment, quick dopamine | TikTok, Instagram Reels, YouTube Shorts, Games | Rapid dopamine spike, insulin-like crash, cravings | 20% max |
| **Fats (Connection)** | Social nourishment, meaningful interaction | iMessage with close friends, FaceTime, family group chats | Oxytocin release, emotional satiety, belonging | 25% of screen time |
| **Fiber (Learning)** | Slow-digestion information, skill building | Kindle, Podcasts, Educational YouTube, News | Sustained attention, neuroplasticity, growth | 25% of screen time |

**API Implementation:**
- `FamilyActivityPicker` selection → User categorizes each app into macronutrient (not automatic—builds awareness)
- `DeviceActivityMonitor` tracks daily macro ratios
- `ShieldConfiguration` shows "Nutritional Facts" label when opening apps: "This app is high in Carbs (89%)"

